Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Check Digits", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"Section5c"],

Cell["\<\
We conclude this section with a function to check the validity of an ISBN. \
Recall that the ISBN-10 code consists of 10 digits, the last of which is \
computed by the formula \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[Cell[TextData[{
   Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["x", "10"], "="}], TraditionalForm]]],
   Cell[BoxData[
    FormBox[
     RowBox[{
      UnderoverscriptBox[
       StyleBox["\[Sum]",
        FontSize->16], 
       StyleBox[
        RowBox[{"i", "=", "1"}], "TI"], "9",
       LimitsPositioning->False], 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"i", "\[CenterDot]", 
        SubscriptBox["x", "i"]}], " ", 
       RowBox[{"(", 
        RowBox[{"mod", " ", "11"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineMath"]
  }]], TraditionalForm]], "Equation"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The symbol X is used in case ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["x", "10"], "=", "10"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Our ",
 StyleBox["checkISBN", "Input"],
 " function will accept the ISBN as a string. It is necessary that we use \
strings in case the ISBN contains X as the check digit. Consider the ISBN \
below."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"isbnExample", "=", "\"\<0073383090\>\""}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"0073383090\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648117829256*^9, 3.5806482402717657`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", in order to access a character within a string, you use the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringTake",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringTake"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function. The first argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringTake",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringTake"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is the string. In order to obtain a single character, you provide as the \
second argument a list with the position of the character as the sole \
element. For example, the third character of our example is obtained as \
follows."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
  RowBox[{"isbnExample", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", "3", "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"7\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482403385878`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Note that the output is still a string, however.", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
   RowBox[{"isbnExample", ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", "3", "}"}]}], "]"}], "//", "FullForm"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  StyleBox["\"\<7\>\"",
   ShowSpecialCharacters->False,
   ShowStringCharacters->True,
   NumberMarks->True],
  FullForm]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482403872004`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In order to perform arithmetic, we need to turn the character into an \
integer. To do this, we can use the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ToExpression",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ToExpression"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function. When ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ToExpression",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ToExpression"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is applied to a string, ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " interprets the string as ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " input. In this example, the string \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]7\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] will be interpreted as if we had entered 7 on an \
input line. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ToExpression", "[", 
   RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
    RowBox[{"isbnExample", ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", "3", "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}], "//", "FullForm"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  StyleBox["7",
   ShowSpecialCharacters->False,
   ShowStringCharacters->True,
   NumberMarks->True],
  FullForm]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648240420834*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Conversely, the function ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ToString",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ToString"], "FunctionLink"],
 " will convert an expression into a string. The following converts the \
number 7 into the string \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]7\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ToString", "[", "7", "]"}], "//", "FullForm"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 TagBox[
  StyleBox["\"\<7\>\"",
   ShowSpecialCharacters->False,
   ShowStringCharacters->True,
   NumberMarks->True],
  FullForm]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648240472189*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Our function will compute the sum indicated by the formula above using ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Sum",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Sum"], "FunctionLink"],
 ". Recall that the first argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Sum",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Sum"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is an expression in terms of an index variable and the second argument is \
the range for the variable. Once the value of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["x", "10"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is determined, we compare it to the check digit. This is only slightly \
complicated by the fact that a check digit of 10 corresponds to the symbol X. \
Note that we must compare the check digit with the value of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["x", "10"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as strings in both the case that the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["x", "10"], "=", "10"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and when ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["x", "10"], "<", "10"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". This is because the last digit may be X, whether it should be or not, and \
applying the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ToExpression",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ToExpression"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function to the string \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]X\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
will result in the symbol ",
 StyleBox["X", "Input"],
 ", which may in fact be assigned to an expression."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"checkISBN", "[", "isbn_String", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"i", ",", "check"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"check", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Mod", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"Sum", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"i", "*", 
           RowBox[{"ToExpression", "[", 
            RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
             RowBox[{"isbn", ",", 
              RowBox[{"{", "i", "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", 
           RowBox[{"i", ",", "9"}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ",", "11"}], "]"}]}], ";",
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"Which", "[", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"check", "<", "10"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"ToString", "[", "check", "]"}], "\[Equal]", 
        RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
         RowBox[{"isbn", ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", "10", "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{"check", "\[Equal]", "10"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
         RowBox[{"isbn", ",", 
          RowBox[{"{", "10", "}"}]}], "]"}], "\[Equal]", "\"\<X\>\""}]}], 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Recall that a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Which",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Which"], "FunctionLink"],
 " statement evaluates the first argument and if it is true, returns the \
value of the second argument. If the first argument is false, then it moves \
to the third argument and evaluates that test, and so on."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"checkISBN", "[", "isbnExample", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482405503273`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"checkISBN", "[", "\"\<084930149X\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["False"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482405877743`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"checkISBN", "[", "\"\<232150031X\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["True"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482406394377`*^9}]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["4.6 Cryptography", "Section",
 CellTags->"Section6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In this, the final section of Chapter 4, we will see how ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " can be used to encode and decode strings using two of the approaches \
described in the textbook. Specifically, we will see how to implement a \
classical affine cypher and the RSA system. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Encoding Strings", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"Section6a"],

Cell["\<\
Before we can implement the encryption algorithms, we need to encode strings \
as numbers. In this manual, we will deviate slightly from the convention used \
in the textbook. Instead of assigning the letter A to 0, B to 1, and so on \
with Z assigned to 25, we will assign the space character to 0, A to 1, B to \
2, and so on with Z set to 26. We will then work modulo 27 instead of 26. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Some Functions for Working with Strings", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " contains a variety of functions for working with strings. We have already \
seen, in the previous section, the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringTake",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringTake"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function. This function is used to obtain a substring of a given string. \
Its first argument is the initial string, and the second argument specifies \
what part of the string to return. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If the second argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringTake",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringTake"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is a positive integer, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the result will be the first ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " characters. For example, the following produces the first 5 characters in \
the string \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]The quick brown fox\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<The quick brown fox\>\"", ",", "5"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"The q\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648240687872*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If the second argument is a negative integer, say ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"-", "n"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the result is the final ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " characters."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<The quick brown fox\>\"", ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", "5"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"n fox\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648240737556*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
To obtain the character in a particular location, as was done in Section 4.5, \
the second argument is given as a list containing the desired position. For \
example, to obtain the character in position 8, enter the following.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<The quick brown fox\>\"", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", "8", "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"c\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648240770713*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "With a pair of integers in a list given as the second argument, ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringTake",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringTake"], "FunctionLink"],
 " returns the substring between the given two positions. The following \
produces the substring consisting of characters 5 through 8."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringTake", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<The quick brown fox\>\"", ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"5", ",", "8"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"quic\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648240821969*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ToUpperCase",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ToUpperCase"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function makes all of the letters in its input string upper case."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"ToUpperCase", "[", "\"\<The quick brown fox\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"THE QUICK BROWN FOX\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648241001553*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ToUpperCase",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ToUpperCase"], "FunctionLink"],
 " command is useful in this context because it means we only have to work \
with the 26 uppercase letters and the space character instead of the full 53 \
characters, including both upper and lower case letters and space."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The next function we will need is the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Characters",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Characters"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function and its inverse ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringJoin",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringJoin"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["<>", "Input"],
 "). The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Characters",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Characters"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function takes a string and returns a list of characters."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Characters", "[", "\"\<THE QUICK BROWN FOX\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"\<\"T\"\>", ",", "\<\"H\"\>", ",", "\<\"E\"\>", ",", "\<\" \"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"Q\"\>", ",", "\<\"U\"\>", ",", "\<\"I\"\>", ",", "\<\"C\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"K\"\>", ",", "\<\" \"\>", ",", "\<\"B\"\>", ",", "\<\"R\"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"O\"\>", ",", "\<\"W\"\>", ",", "\<\"N\"\>", ",", "\<\" \"\>", 
   ",", "\<\"F\"\>", ",", "\<\"O\"\>", ",", "\<\"X\"\>"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482410592127`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringJoin",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringJoin"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function does the opposite. Given two or more strings as arguments, it \
joins them into one string."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringJoin", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<The quick\>\"", ",", "\"\< \>\"", ",", "\"\<brown fox\>\""}], 
  "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"The quick brown fox\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648241092537*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["StringJoin",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/StringJoin"], "FunctionLink"],
 " also has an operator form, ",
 StyleBox["<>", "Input"],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"\"\<The quick\>\"", " ", "<>", " ", "\"\< \>\"", " ", "<>", " ", 
  "\"\<brown fox\>\""}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"The quick brown fox\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648241153698*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
It can also accept a list of strings as its arguments and will return the \
string formed by joining the members of the list. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"StringJoin", "[", 
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{
   "\"\<T\>\"", ",", "\"\<H\>\"", ",", "\"\<E\>\"", ",", "\"\< \>\"", ",", 
    "\"\<Q\>\"", ",", "\"\<U\>\"", ",", "\"\<I\>\"", ",", "\"\<C\>\"", ",", 
    "\"\<K\>\"", ",", "\"\< \>\"", ",", "\"\<B\>\"", ",", "\"\<R\>\"", ",", 
    "\"\<O\>\"", ",", "\"\<W\>\"", ",", "\"\<N\>\"", ",", "\"\< \>\"", ",", 
    "\"\<F\>\"", ",", "\"\<O\>\"", ",", "\"\<X\>\""}], "}"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"THE QUICK BROWN FOX\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482411918592`*^9}]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Mapping Characters to Integers", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To represent the function that maps characters to integers, and its \
inverse, we will use two indexed variables, ",
 StyleBox["charToNum", "Input"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["numToChar", "Input"],
 ". In the ",
 StyleBox["charToNum", "Input"],
 " variable, the space character and capital letters will serve as the \
indices with the corresponding integers the entries. The ",
 StyleBox["numToChar", "Input"],
 " variable will be the reverse. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To define these two indexed variables, rather than entering each individual \
assignment manually, we will apply the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["MapThread",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/MapThread"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function. Recall that ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["MapThread",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/MapThread"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is used to apply a function to lists of arguments. The first argument to \
",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["MapThread",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/MapThread"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is a function. In this case, we will create a pure function that sets a \
value of the indexed variable. The second argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["MapThread",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/MapThread"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is a list of lists, with the inner lists containing the arguments. The \
elements of the first sublist are values of the first argument of the \
function, the elements of the second sublist values of the second argument \
and so forth. The result of ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["MapThread",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/MapThread"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is that the function is evaluated on corresponding pairs of elements from \
the lists."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here, ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["MapThread",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/MapThread"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is used to define ",
 StyleBox["charToNum", "Input"],
 ". Note that the parentheses are needed in the pure function, since ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Function",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Function"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["&", "Input"],
 ") has greater precedence than ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Set",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Set"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["=", "Input"],
 ")."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"MapThread", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"charToNum", "[", "#1", "]"}], "=", "#2"}], ")"}], "&"}], ",", 
    
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Characters", "[", "\"\< ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\>\"", "]"}],
       ",", 
      RowBox[{"Range", "[", 
       RowBox[{"0", ",", "26"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "After executing this ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["MapThread",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/MapThread"], "FunctionLink"],
 " expression, ",
 StyleBox["charToNum", "Input"],
 ", issued with a character as an index, returns the appropriate value."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"charToNum", "[", "\"\<F\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["6"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648241300215*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox["numToChar", "Input"],
 " indexed variable is created in the same way, but reversed."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"MapThread", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"numToChar", "[", "#1", "]"}], "=", "#2"}], ")"}], "&"}], ",", 
    
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Range", "[", 
       RowBox[{"0", ",", "26"}], "]"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{
      "Characters", "[", "\"\< ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\>\"", "]"}]}], 
     "}"}]}], "]"}], ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"numToChar", "[", "6", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"F\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648241368133*^9}]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Converting Between a String and a Numerical Representation", \
"Subsubsection"],

Cell["\<\
We now have the tools needed to encode a string as a list of numbers and a \
decode the numerical representation as a string. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In the ",
 StyleBox["stringToNums", "Input"],
 " function, we will first apply ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["ToUpperCase",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/ToUpperCase"], "FunctionLink"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Characters",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Characters"], "FunctionLink"],
 " to produce a list of uppercase characters. Then we will use the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Map",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Map"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["/@", "Input"],
 ") function to apply the ",
 StyleBox["charToNum", "Input"],
 " table to each character. When ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Map",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Map"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["/@", "Input"],
 ") is applied to a function (in this case a function represented by an \
indexed variable) and a list, it returns the list obtained by applying the \
function to each element of the list. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"stringToNums", "[", "s_String", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", "charList", "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"charList", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Characters", "[", 
       RowBox[{"ToUpperCase", "[", "s", "]"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"Map", "[", 
      RowBox[{"charToNum", ",", "charList"}], "]"}]}]}], 
   "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"stringToNums", "[", "\"\<The quick brown fox\>\"", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "20", ",", "8", ",", "5", ",", "0", ",", "17", ",", "21", ",", "9", ",", 
   "3", ",", "11", ",", "0", ",", "2", ",", "18", ",", "15", ",", "23", ",", 
   "14", ",", "0", ",", "6", ",", "15", ",", "24"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.58064824143512*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox["numsToString", "Input"],
 " function begins with a list of integers and returns the string."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"numsToString", "[", 
   RowBox[{"numList", ":", 
    RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", "charList", "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"charList", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Map", "[", 
       RowBox[{"numToChar", ",", "numList"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"StringJoin", "[", "charList", "]"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"numsToString", "[", 
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{
   "8", ",", "5", ",", "12", ",", "12", ",", "15", ",", "0", ",", "23", ",", 
    "15", ",", "18", ",", "12", ",", "4"}], "}"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"HELLO WORLD\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482415158463`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Now that we have the ability to convert strings into a numerical \
representation and back again, we are ready to implement our encryption \
algorithms. \
\>", "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Classical Cryptography", "Subsection",
 PageBreakWithin->False,
 CellTags->"Section6b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We will now implement an affine cipher in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". Recall from the text that a general affine cipher has the form"
}], "Text",
 PageBreakAbove->False,
 PageBreakWithin->False],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"f", "(", "p", ")"}], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"a", "\[CenterDot]", "p"}], "+", "b"}], ")"}], " ", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"mod", " ", "27"}], ")"}], Cell[""]}]}], 
  TraditionalForm]], "Equation"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is an integer corresponding to a character that is to be encrypted. We \
will refer to the pair ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " as the key to the cipher. For decryption to be feasible, the key must be \
chosen so that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["f", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a bijection. This amounts to choosing an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 " that is relatively prime to 27. (Note that the text uses a modulus of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["26", TraditionalForm]]],
 " where we use ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["27", TraditionalForm]]],
 " because we are considering space to be an encodable character.) "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Encrypting a string requires three simple steps. First, the string is \
transformed into its numerical representation via ",
 StyleBox["stringToNums", "Input"],
 ". Second, the function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["f", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is applied to each number. And third, the ",
 StyleBox["numsToString", "Input"],
 " function transforms the result back into a string. Our ",
 StyleBox["affineCipher", "Input"],
 " function accepts as input a string and values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["b", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We ensure that the argument ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is relatively prime to 27 by imposing a ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Condition",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Condition"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["/;", "Input"],
 ") and creating a message if it is not. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"affineCipher", "::", "arga"}], "=", 
   "\"\<Second argument must be relatively prime to 27.\>\""}], 
  ";"}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Recall that following the name and arguments of a function definition with \
the condition operator ",
 StyleBox["/;", "Input"],
 " and an expression that results in true or false allows you to create \
functions that will not execute on invalid arguments."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"affineCipher", "[", 
    RowBox[{"s_String", ",", "a_Integer", ",", "b_Integer"}], "]"}], "/;", 
   RowBox[{"If", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"CoprimeQ", "[", 
      RowBox[{"a", ",", "27"}], "]"}], ",", "True", ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"Message", "[", 
       RowBox[{"affineCipher", "::", "arga"}], "]"}], ";", "False"}]}], 
    "]"}]}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"S", ",", "T"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"S", "=", 
      RowBox[{"stringToNums", "[", "s", "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"T", "=", 
      RowBox[{"Map", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"Mod", "[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"a", "*", "#"}], "+", "b"}], ",", "27"}], "]"}], "&"}], 
        ",", "S"}], "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"numsToString", "[", "T", "]"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note the use of ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Map",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Map"], "FunctionLink"],
 " (",
 StyleBox["/@", "Input"],
 ") to apply the function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"f", "(", "p", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", defined as a pure function, to each character."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We now use the cipher to encrypt \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]The quick brown fox\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] with the key ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"5", ",", "3"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"affineCipher", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<The quick brown fox\>\"", ",", "5", ",", "3"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"VPACG URDCMLXJSCFXO\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.58064824162071*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To decrypt the message, we use the same function. The discussion following \
Example 4 in Section 4.5 of the text indicates that decrypting amounts to \
solving ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"c", "\[Congruent]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"a", "\[CenterDot]", "p"}], "+", "b"}], ")"}], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "27"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". As the text shows, we obtain ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", "\[Congruent]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox["a", 
       RowBox[{"-", "1"}]], "(", 
      RowBox[{"c", "-", "b"}], ")"}], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "27"}], ")"}]}], "\[Congruent]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox["a", 
       RowBox[{"-", "1"}]], "c"}], "-", 
     RowBox[{
      SuperscriptBox["a", 
       RowBox[{"-", "1"}]], "b", " ", 
      RowBox[{"(", 
       RowBox[{"mod", " ", "27"}], ")"}]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". In other words, to decrypt a message encrypted using the key ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", we use the same procedure but with key ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["a", 
      RowBox[{"-", "1"}]], ",", 
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"-", 
       SuperscriptBox["a", 
        RowBox[{"-", "1"}]]}], "b"}]}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "First, compute the inverse of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"a", "=", "5"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"PowerMod", "[", 
  RowBox[{"5", ",", 
   RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", "27"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["11"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482416537848`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "And then ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"-", 
     SuperscriptBox["a", 
      RowBox[{"-", "1"}]]}], "b"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", being sure to include the negative."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Mod", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"-", "11"}], "*", "3"}], ",", "27"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["21"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648241691242*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Thus the decryption key is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"(", 
    RowBox[{"11", ",", "21"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"affineCipher", "[", 
  RowBox[{"\"\<VPACG URDCMLXJSCFXO\>\"", ",", "11", ",", "21"}], 
  "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData["\<\"THE QUICK BROWN FOX\"\>"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648241720047*^9}]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["RSA Encryption", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"Section6c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We will now see how to use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to implement the RSA cryptosystem. Implementing the RSA system involves \
two steps: key generation and the encryption algorithm. "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
To construct keys in the RSA system, we need to find pairs of large primes, \
say with 200 digits each. Since messages can be decrypted by anyone who can \
factor the product of these primes, the two primes must be large enough so \
that their product is extremely difficult to factor. A 400 digit integer fits \
the bill since factoring requires an extremely large amount of computer time. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Because the use of very large prime numbers would make our examples \
impractical as examples, we shall illustrate the RSA system using smaller \
primes. We will discuss at the end of this section how you can use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to generate large prime numbers. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Key Generation", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first step in key generation is to choose two distinct large prime \
numbers, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". From these, we produce the public key, which consists of the public \
modulus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "=", 
    RowBox[{"p", "\[CenterDot]", "q"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and the public exponent ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["e", TraditionalForm]]],
 " which is relatively prime to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"\[Phi]", "(", "n", ")"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"p", "-", "1"}], ")"}], 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"q", "-", "1"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". We also produce the private key, consisting of the public modulus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and the inverse of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["e", TraditionalForm]]],
 " modulo ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"p", "-", "1"}], ")"}], 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"q", "-", "1"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Since ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["e", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is unrelated to the primes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", it can be generated in a number of ways. For our implementation below, we \
will take ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["e", TraditionalForm]]],
 " to be ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["13", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here is a ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " function to handle key generation. The ",
 StyleBox["generateKeys", "Input"],
 " function accepts as input two prime numbers. It returns a list of two \
lists where the sublists are the public and private keys. That is, it returns \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"n", ",", "e"}], "}"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"n", ",", 
       SuperscriptBox["e", 
        RowBox[{"-", "1"}]]}], "}"}]}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Given the primes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the procedure computes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "=", 
    RowBox[{"p", "\[CenterDot]", "q"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"\[Phi]", "(", "n", ")"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"p", "-", "1"}], ")"}], 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"q", "-", "1"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"d", "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["e", 
      RowBox[{"-", "1"}]], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", 
       RowBox[{"\[Phi]", "(", "n", ")"}]}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"generateKeys", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"p_", "?", "PrimeQ"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"q_", "?", "PrimeQ"}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"n", ",", "phin", ",", "e", ",", "d"}], "}"}], ",", 
    "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"e", "=", "13"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"n", "=", 
      RowBox[{"p", "*", "q"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"phin", "=", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"(", 
        RowBox[{"p", "-", "1"}], ")"}], "*", 
       RowBox[{"(", 
        RowBox[{"q", "-", "1"}], ")"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"d", "=", 
      RowBox[{"PowerMod", "[", 
       RowBox[{"e", ",", 
        RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", "phin"}], "]"}]}], ";", 
     "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"n", ",", "e"}], "}"}], ",", 
       RowBox[{"{", 
        RowBox[{"n", ",", "d"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In a practical RSA implementation, we would likely use some of the \
techniques discussed at the end of this section to incorporate into our ",
 StyleBox["generateKeys", "Input"],
 " procedure the generation of the primes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["q", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", rather than passing them as arguments."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We generate keys using the prime numbers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"p", "=", "59"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"q", "=", "71"}], TraditionalForm]]]
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"keys", "=", 
  RowBox[{"generateKeys", "[", 
   RowBox[{"59", ",", "71"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"4189", ",", "13"}], "}"}], ",", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{"4189", ",", "937"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648117922452*^9, 3.580648241802704*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["The public and private keys are:", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"publickey", "=", 
  RowBox[{"keys", "[", 
   RowBox[{"[", "1", "]"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"4189", ",", "13"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648117947547*^9, 3.580648241862368*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"privatekey", "=", 
  RowBox[{"keys", "[", 
   RowBox[{"[", "2", "]"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"4189", ",", "937"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648117979858*^9, 3.580648241972598*^9}]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Encoding", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Now that we have the keys, we turn to encoding the message. As described in \
the text, we encode the message in much the same way as for affine ciphers, \
except that we block groups of characters into single integers. The block \
length must be chosen so that, after conversion, the largest integer produced \
is less than the modulus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Here, we have ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "=", "4198"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and the largest block that can be produced is 2626 for \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]ZZ\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. "
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
We need to ensure that this part of the process is reversible. Consider the \
string \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]VA\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. This comprises one \
block. Since \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]V\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] has code 22 and \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]A\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] has code 1, it is tempting \
to code \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]VA\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] as 221. But when \
you go to convert this back to a string, it is impossible to tell if it was \
22 and 1 indicating \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]VA\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] or if \
it was 2 and 21, which represents \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]BU\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. To avoid this, we code \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]A\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] as 01. Or, what amounts to the same thing, when we \
compose the block, we multiply the value of the first character by 100. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For a specific example, consider the message \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]SECRET \
MESSAGE\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]. We can use our ",
 StyleBox["stringToNums", "Input"],
 " function from above to get the numeric representation of each character. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"messageString", "=", 
  RowBox[{"stringToNums", "[", "\"\<SECRET MESSAGE\>\"", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "19", ",", "5", ",", "3", ",", "18", ",", "5", ",", "20", ",", "0", ",", 
   "13", ",", "5", ",", "19", ",", "19", ",", "1", ",", "7", ",", "5"}], 
  "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648118013386*^9, 3.580648242010542*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
You can see that the first pair should be encoded as 1905, the second as \
0318, and so on. Note that the extra 0 is unnecessary in second block, since \
0318 and 318 are numerically equal. We can obtain the desired results by \
multiplying the first number in each pair by 100 as follows. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"messageCode", "=", 
  RowBox[{"Table", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{"messageString", "[", 
       RowBox[{"[", 
        RowBox[{"i", "-", "1"}], "]"}], "]"}], "*", "100"}], "+", 
     RowBox[{"messageString", "[", 
      RowBox[{"[", "i", "]"}], "]"}]}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"i", ",", "2", ",", 
      RowBox[{"Length", "[", "messageString", "]"}], ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "1905", ",", "318", ",", "520", ",", "13", ",", "519", ",", "1901", ",", 
   "705"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806481180475283`*^9, 3.5806482420399427`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note that the final 2 in the list describing the table variable ",
 StyleBox["i", "Input"],
 " indicates that the variable should be increased by 2 each iteration."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Encryption", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The encryption algorithm will take as input this list of integers and the \
public key. Each message block ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["m", "i"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is transformed into a ciphertext block ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["c", "i"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " with the function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"C", "\[Congruent]", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["M", "e"], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "n"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"RSA", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"n_Integer", ",", "e_Integer"}], "}"}], ",", 
    RowBox[{"msg", ":", 
     RowBox[{"{", "__Integer", "}"}]}]}], "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", "c", "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{"c", "=", 
     RowBox[{"Map", "[", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"PowerMod", "[", 
         RowBox[{"#", ",", "e", ",", "n"}], "]"}], "&"}], ",", "msg"}], 
      "]"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Observe how the arguments for this function were defined. The function ",
 StyleBox["RSA", "Input"],
 " accepts a list consisting of two integers named ",
 StyleBox["n", "Input"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["e", "Input"],
 " and a list of integers called ",
 StyleBox["msg", "Input"],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell["\<\
Our \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]SECRET MESSAGE\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is \
encrypted as\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"cipherText", "=", 
  RowBox[{"RSA", "[", 
   RowBox[{"publickey", ",", "messageCode"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "723", ",", "3360", ",", "2306", ",", "1979", ",", "2695", ",", "917", ",", 
   "1863"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648118081271*^9, 3.580648242123251*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Decryption is accomplished by applying the same algorithm with the private \
decryption key. \
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"RSA", "[", 
  RowBox[{"privatekey", ",", "cipherText"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "1905", ",", "318", ",", "520", ",", "13", ",", "519", ",", "1901", ",", 
   "705"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648242177787*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note that the result is identical to ",
 StyleBox["messageCode", "Input"],
 " and it can be decoded into the message \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]SECRET \
MESSAGE\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Generating Large Primes", "Subsubsection"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If you were to use small primes, as we did in the example, there would be \
no real security. Anyone could factor ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the product of the primes, and then could compute the decrypting key ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["d", TraditionalForm]]],
 " from the encrypting key ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["e", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Using ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "'s computational abilities, we can generate fairly large prime numbers for \
use in an RSA key. Remember that what is needed is a pair of prime numbers, \
each of about 200 digits. Moreover, they should be selected in an \
unpredictable fashion. To do this in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", we can use the ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["RandomPrime",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/RandomPrime"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The first argument to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["RandomPrime",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/RandomPrime"], "FunctionLink"],
 " is either an integer, in which case the function returns a prime up to \
that value, or a list of two integers, in which case the prime generated will \
be between them. The function can also accept a second argument in order to \
produce more than one at a time. For example, with second argument 2, the \
function will return a list of two primes."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Of course, the primes generated by ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["RandomPrime",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/RandomPrime"], "FunctionLink"],
 " are in fact pseudorandom, not truly random."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To produce two random primes with between 200 and 300 digits, we call ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["RandomPrime",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/RandomPrime"], "FunctionLink"],
 " as follows."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"RandomPrime", "[", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"10", "^", "200"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"10", "^", "300"}]}], "}"}], ",", "2"}], "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "203811282225624478078515012702649786138281496527439371700973612663525005733\
829808284691085575889803229552341487758547691682881193084510110939501440934750\
105320461906995334938382416772382434499456075519109653526335197080906586381573\
712759700848157253301804288748583902501712285961052463578025183343447", ",", 
   "59365546738754022436340641450864883587732512591348579073759119314307629192\
350532985495885848595791014292128024112807121899383404220166868674425680721962\
309072738952130066966126911805826586497758986222947673932573148571101045565016\
262106458575853970966783858288253877707789607325765303266287865624013"}], 
  "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806482423232927`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "It is left to the reader to incorporate these ideas in improved versions of \
the ",
 StyleBox["generateKeys", "Input"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox["RSA", "Input"],
 " functions."
}], "Text"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Solutions to Computer Projects and Computations and Explorations\
\>", "Section",
 PageBreakWithin->False,
 CellTags->"Solutions"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Computer Projects 3", "Subsection",
 PageBreakAbove->False,
 PageBreakWithin->False,
 CellTags->"SolutionsCP3"],

Cell["\<\
Given a positive integer, find the Cantor expansion of this integer (see the \
preamble to Exercise 48 of Section 4.2).\
\>", "Problem"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Solution:",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " Recall the definition of the Cantor expansion. Given an integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the Cantor expansion of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{"a", "=", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "n"], 
     RowBox[{"n", "!"}]}], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["a", 
       RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}]], "(", 
      RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}], ")"}], "!"}], "+", "\[CenterEllipsis]", "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "2"], 
     RowBox[{"2", "!"}]}], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "1"], 
     RowBox[{"1", "!"}]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "Equation"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Observe that every term except for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["a", "1"], 
    RowBox[{"1", "!"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is divisible by 2. That is, "
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "n"], 
     RowBox[{"n", "!"}]}], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["a", 
       RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}]], "(", 
      RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}], ")"}], "!"}], "+", "\[CenterEllipsis]", "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "2"], 
     RowBox[{"2", "!"}]}], "+", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "1"], 
     RowBox[{"1", "!"}], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "2"}], ")"}]}]}], "=", 
   SubscriptBox["a", "1"]}], TraditionalForm]], "Equation"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "So set ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["a", "1"], "=", 
    RowBox[{"a", " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "2"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". And let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["y", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " be the remainder with the 2 divided out. In other words, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["y", "1"], "=", 
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{"a", "-", 
      SubscriptBox["a", "1"]}], "2"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", or"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 FormBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["y", "1"], "=", 
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["a", "n"], 
       RowBox[{"n", "!"}]}], "+", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{
        SubscriptBox["a", 
         RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}]], "(", 
        RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}], ")"}], "!"}], "+", "\[CenterEllipsis]", "+", 
      
      RowBox[{
       SubscriptBox["a", "2"], 
       RowBox[{"2", "!"}]}]}], "2"], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["a", "n"], 
      FractionBox[
       RowBox[{"n", "!"}], "2"]}], "+", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["a", 
       RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}]], 
      FractionBox[
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"(", 
         RowBox[{"n", "-", "1"}], ")"}], "!"}], "2"]}], "+", 
     "\[CenterEllipsis]", "+", 
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["a", "3"], "3"}], "+", 
     SubscriptBox["a", "2"]}]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "Equation"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Now every term other than the last contains a factor of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["3", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", so set ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["a", "2"], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["y", "1"], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "3"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and let ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["y", "2"], "=", 
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["y", "1"], "-", 
      SubscriptBox["a", "2"]}], "3"]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In general, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["a", "k"], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["y", 
      RowBox[{"k", "-", "1"}]], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"mod", " ", "k"}], "+", "1"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["y", "k"], "=", 
    FractionBox[
     RowBox[{
      SubscriptBox["y", 
       RowBox[{"k", "-", "1"}]], "-", 
      SubscriptBox["a", "k"]}], 
     RowBox[{"k", "+", "1"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". It is left to the reader to verify that this process produces the Cantor \
expansion of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The algorithm described above leads to the function below which accepts a \
positive integer as input and returns a list of integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "1"], ",", 
     SubscriptBox["a", "2"], ",", "\[Ellipsis]", ",", 
     SubscriptBox["a", "n"]}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"cantorExpansion", "[", "n_Integer", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"a", ",", 
      RowBox[{"k", "=", "1"}], ",", 
      RowBox[{"y", "=", "n"}]}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Reap", "[", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"While", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"y", "\[NotEqual]", "0"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"a", "=", 
          RowBox[{"Mod", "[", 
           RowBox[{"y", ",", 
            RowBox[{"k", "+", "1"}]}], "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
         RowBox[{"Sow", "[", "a", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
         RowBox[{"y", "=", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"(", 
            RowBox[{"y", "-", "a"}], ")"}], "/", 
           RowBox[{"(", 
            RowBox[{"k", "+", "1"}], ")"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
         RowBox[{"k", "++"}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], 
      "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], "[", 
     RowBox[{"[", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"cantorExpansion", "[", "471", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{"1", ",", "1", ",", "2", ",", "4", ",", "3"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648242367312*^9}]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Computer Projects 21", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"SolutionsCP21"],

Cell["\<\
Generate a shared key using the Diffie-Hellman key exchange protocol.\
\>", "Problem"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Solution:",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " Recall from Section 4.6 of the text the Diffie-Hellman key exchange \
protocol. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(1) Alice and Bob agree on a prime number ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and a primitive root ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 " of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". For this example, we\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll use a relatively small prime, \
one with between 6 and 8 digits."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"dhPrime", "=", 
  RowBox[{"RandomPrime", "[", 
   RowBox[{"{", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"10", "^", "6"}], ",", 
     RowBox[{"10", "^", "8"}]}], "}"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData["6397837"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806481181301003`*^9, 3.580648242408729*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For the primitive root, we\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll use ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["PrimitiveRoot",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/PrimitiveRoot"], "FunctionLink"],
 " to get the smallest primitive root of the prime."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"dhRoot", "=", 
  RowBox[{"PrimitiveRoot", "[", "dhPrime", "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData["2"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806481181638727`*^9, 3.5806482424400063`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(2) Alice chooses a secret integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Let's choose 421 since this is Computer Project 21 in Chapter 4. We need \
to compute  ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["a", 
     SubscriptBox["k", "1"]], "(", 
    RowBox[{"mod", " ", "p"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and send the resulting value to Bob. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"aliceSends", "=", 
  RowBox[{"PowerMod", "[", 
   RowBox[{"dhRoot", ",", "421", ",", "dhPrime"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData["792900"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648118197822*^9, 3.5806482424759283`*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note that we use ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["PowerMod",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/PowerMod"], "FunctionLink"],
 " so that the exponentiation is computed efficiently."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(3) Bob also chooses a secret integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". From the perspective of Alice, we won't know what value of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " that Bob chooses, only the value of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["a", 
     SubscriptBox["k", "2"]], "(", 
    RowBox[{"mod", " ", "p"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". So we'll have ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " choose ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " randomly in the computation. "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"bobSends", "=", 
  RowBox[{"PowerMod", "[", 
   RowBox[{"dhRoot", ",", 
    RowBox[{"RandomInteger", "[", 
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"1", ",", "dhPrime"}], "}"}], "]"}], ",", "dhPrime"}], 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData["6220184"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648118230483*^9, 3.580648242524972*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["RandomInteger",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/RandomInteger"], "FunctionLink"],
 " function has similar syntax to ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["RandomPrime",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/RandomPrime"], "FunctionLink"],
 ". Given a list of two integers as its argument, it returns a pseudorandom \
integer between the two integers. There are no necessary restrictions on the \
values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", however, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["a", 
     RowBox[{"k", "+", "p"}]], "\[Congruent]", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["a", "k"], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "p"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", so it is no loss to assume the integers lie between 1 and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(4) and (5) Alice computes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox[
     RowBox[{"(", 
      SuperscriptBox["a", 
       SubscriptBox["k", "2"]], ")"}], 
     SubscriptBox["k", "1"]], " ", 
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"mod", " ", "p"}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " using the result that Bob transmitted and her ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "1"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Bob does the same using the value he got from Alice and his secret ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["k", "2"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". "
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"sharedKey", "=", 
  RowBox[{"PowerMod", "[", 
   RowBox[{"bobSends", ",", "421", ",", "dhPrime"}], "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[BoxData["2357094"], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.5806481182641697`*^9, 3.580648242559403*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
At the conclusion, both Alice and Bob know this shared key, but no one else \
does. \
\>", "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Computations and Explorations 1", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"SolutionsCE1"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Determine whether ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["2", "p"], "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is prime for each of the primes not exceeding 100."
}], "Problem"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Solution:",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " To solve this problem, we will write a ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " program that tests each prime ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " less than or equal to a given value to see whether ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["2", "p"], "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a Mersenne prime. The function will output a list of those primes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for which ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["2", "p"], "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is prime."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"checkMersenne", "[", "max_Integer", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", 
     RowBox[{"p", "=", "2"}], "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Reap", "[", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"While", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"p", "\[LessEqual]", "max"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{
         RowBox[{"If", "[", 
          RowBox[{
           RowBox[{"PrimeQ", "[", 
            RowBox[{
             RowBox[{"2", "^", "p"}], "-", "1"}], "]"}], ",", 
           RowBox[{"Sow", "[", "p", "]"}]}], "]"}], ";", 
         "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
         RowBox[{"p", "=", 
          RowBox[{"NextPrime", "[", "p", "]"}]}]}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
       "]"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], "[", 
     RowBox[{"[", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The primes ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " less than 100 such that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["2", "p"], "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is prime are:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"checkMersenne", "[", "100", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "2", ",", "3", ",", "5", ",", "7", ",", "13", ",", "17", ",", "19", ",", 
   "31", ",", "61", ",", "89"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648242622334*^9}]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "It is of note that there is a better test, called the Lucas-Lehmer test, \
that is more efficient than ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["PrimeQ",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/PrimeQ"], "FunctionLink"],
 " for checking primality of numbers of the form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["2", "p"], "-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and can be implemented in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ". For a complete description of that algorithm, consult Rosen's text on \
Number Theory. "
}], "Text"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Computations and Explorations 5", "Subsection",
 CellTags->"SolutionsCE5"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Find as many primes of the form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["n", "2"], "+", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " where ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a positive integer as you can. It is not known whether there are \
infinitely many such primes."
}], "Problem"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Solution:",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " We write a ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " function that, given a maximum ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", tests the integers of the given form."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"ce5", "[", "max_Integer", "]"}], ":=", 
  RowBox[{"Module", "[", 
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"{", "n", "}"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"Reap", "[", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
      RowBox[{"For", "[", 
       RowBox[{
        RowBox[{"n", "=", "1"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"n", "\[LessEqual]", "max"}], ",", 
        RowBox[{"n", "++"}], ",", "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
        RowBox[{"If", "[", 
         RowBox[{
          RowBox[{"PrimeQ", "[", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"n", "^", "2"}], "+", "1"}], "]"}], ",", 
          RowBox[{"Sow", "[", 
           RowBox[{
            RowBox[{"n", "^", "2"}], "+", "1"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}], 
       "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", "]"}], "[", 
     RowBox[{"[", 
      RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "\[IndentingNewLine]", 
   "]"}]}]], "Input",
 InitializationCell->True],

Cell[TextData[{
 "To save space, we\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll only compute up to a maximum of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "=", "100"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "."
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"ce5", "[", "100", "]"}]], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"{", 
  RowBox[{
  "2", ",", "5", ",", "17", ",", "37", ",", "101", ",", "197", ",", "257", 
   ",", "401", ",", "577", ",", "677", ",", "1297", ",", "1601", ",", "2917", 
   ",", "3137", ",", "4357", ",", "5477", ",", "7057", ",", "8101", ",", 
   "8837"}], "}"}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.580648242672176*^9}]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Exercises", "Section",
 CellTags->"Exercises"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to generate the list of the first 100 prime numbers larger than one \
million."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to find the one's complement of an arbitrary integer (see the prelude to \
Exercise 34 of Section 4.2)."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "For which odd prime moduli is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"-", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " a square? That is, for which prime numbers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " does there exist an integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " such that ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["x", "2"], "\[Congruent]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"-", "1"}], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "p"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "?"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to determine which numbers are perfect squares modulo ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for various values of the modulus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". For each perfect square ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["s", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", determine how many square roots ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["s", TraditionalForm]]],
 " has. That is, for how many values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["x", "2"], "\[Congruent]", 
    RowBox[{"s", " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "n"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". What conjectures can you make about the number of different square roots \
an integer has modulo ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "? (The ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " functions ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["PowerMod",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/PowerMod"], "FunctionLink"],
 " and ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["Solve",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/Solve"], "FunctionLink"],
 " may be of use.)"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to find the base 2 expansion of the 4th Fermat number ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["F", "4"], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     SuperscriptBox["2", 
      SuperscriptBox["2", "4"]], "+", "1"}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Do the following for several large integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Compute the time required to calculate the remainder modulo ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 " of various bases ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["b", TraditionalForm]]],
 " raised to the power ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " (that is, to calculate ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["b", 
     SubscriptBox["F", "4"]], "(", 
    RowBox[{"mod", " ", "n"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ") using two different methods. First, do the calculation by a \
straightforward exponentiation. Second, do it using the binary expansion of \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " with repeated squaring and multiplications. Why do you think ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["F", "4"], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is a good choice for the public exponent in the RSA encryption scheme? "
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Modify the function ",
 StyleBox["generateKeys", "Input"],
 " that we developed to produce the keys for the RSA system to incorporate \
the techniques for generating random large primes. Make your procedure take \
as an argument a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]security\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
parameter which measures the number of digits in the primes."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Write ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " functions to encode and decode English sentences into lists of integers, \
appropriate for encryption with RSA. You may ignore punctuation and insist \
that all letters are uppercase. Your functions should accept as input the \
block size."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "There are infinitely many primes of the form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"4", "n"}], "+", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and infinitely many of the form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"4", "n"}], "+", "3"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to determine for various values of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " whether there are more primes of the form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"4", "n"}], "+", "1"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " less than ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["x", TraditionalForm]]],
 " than there are of the form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"4", "n"}], "+", "3"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". What conjectures can you make from this evidence?"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Develop a function for determining whether Mersenne numbers are prime using \
the Lucas-Lehmer test as described in number theory books, such as ",
 StyleBox["Elementary Number Theory and its Applications",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " by K. Rosen. How many Mersenne numbers can you test for primality using ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "? "
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Repunits",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " are integers with decimal expansions consisting entirely of 1s (e.g., 11, \
111, 1111, etc.). Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to factor repunits. How many prime repunits can you find? Explore the same \
question for repunits in different base expansions."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Compute the sequence of pseudorandom numbers generated by the linear \
congruential generator ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["x", 
     RowBox[{"n", "+", "1"}]], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{
       RowBox[{"a", "\[CenterDot]", 
        SubscriptBox["x", "n"]}], "+", "c"}], ")"}], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "m"}], ")"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " for various values of the multiplier ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["a", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", the increment ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["c", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", and the modulus ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 ". For which values do you get a period of length ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for the sequence that you generate? Formulate a conjecture."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " function ",
 StyleBox[ButtonBox["DivisorSigma",
  BaseStyle->"Link",
  ButtonData->"paclet:ref/DivisorSigma"], "FunctionLink"],
 " implements the function defined, for all positive integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", by: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "k"], "(", "n", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the sum of the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["k", TraditionalForm]]],
 "th powers of the positive divisors of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", i.e., ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "k"], "(", "n", ")"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     UnderscriptBox[
      StyleBox["\[Sum]",
       FontSize->16], 
      RowBox[{"d", "|", "n"}],
      LimitsPositioning->False], " ", 
     SuperscriptBox["d", "k"]}]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". For ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"k", "=", "0"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", "n", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is the number of positive divisors of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", which is sometimes also denoted ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[Tau]", "(", "n", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to study the function ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". What conjectures can you make about it? For example, when is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", "n", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " odd? Is there a formula for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", "n", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "? For which integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 " does ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", "n", ")"}], "=", "m"}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 " have a solution for some integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]]],
 "? Is there a formula for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", 
    RowBox[{"m", "\[CenterDot]", "n"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " in terms of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", "m", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", "n", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 "? (Note: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["\[Sigma]", "0"], "(", "n", ")"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is computed by ",
 StyleBox["DivisorSigma[0,n]", "Input"],
 ".)"
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A sequence ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["a", "1"], ",", 
    SubscriptBox["a", "2"], ",", 
    SubscriptBox["a", "3"], ",", "\[Ellipsis]"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " is called ",
 StyleBox["periodic",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " if there are positive integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["N", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for which ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SubscriptBox["a", "n"], "=", 
    SubscriptBox["a", 
     RowBox[{"n", "+", "p"}]]}], TraditionalForm]]],
 " for all ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "\[GreaterEqual]", "N"}], TraditionalForm]]],
 ". The least integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["p", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for which this is true is called the ",
 StyleBox["period",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " of the sequence. The sequence is said to be ",
 StyleBox["periodic modulo",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", for a positive integer ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", if the sequence ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "1"], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "m"}], ")"}]}], ",", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "2"], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "m"}], ")"}]}], ",", 
    RowBox[{
     SubscriptBox["a", "3"], " ", 
     RowBox[{"(", 
      RowBox[{"mod", " ", "m"}], ")"}], "\[Ellipsis]"}]}], 
   TraditionalForm]]],
 " is periodic. Use ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to determine whether the Fibonacci sequence is periodic modulo ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 " for various integers ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and, if so, find the period. Can you, by examining enough different values \
of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 ", make any conjectures concerning the relationship between ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["m", TraditionalForm]]],
 " and the period? Do the same thing for other sequences that you find \
interesting."
}], "Item1Exercise"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "(Class project) The Data Encryption Standard (DES) specifies a widely used \
algorithm for private key cryptography. Find a description of this algorithm \
(for example, in ",
 StyleBox["Cryptography, Theory and Practice",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " by Douglas Stinson). Implement the DES in ",
 StyleBox["Mathematica.",
  FontSlant->"Italic"]
}], "Item1Exercise"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Closed]]
}, Open  ]]
},
